Code for finding dense subgraphs of the type of the paper in Spire2012 
"Compression of Web and Social graphs via dense subgraphs":

The code uses boost_1_40_0 (accesible at http://www.boost.org/doc/libs/1_40_0/more/getting_started/unix-variants.html)
I mainly use it for getting hashes. 

1. First download boost and install it on a directory build it according the information on the same page you download it.

2. Compile and use dense subgraph discovery on dsextract directory

	- Modify the Makefile to set the boost directory
        - compile: make
	- executable: vnmextract: contains code to extract dense subgraphs and store them on disk
        Parameters: graph_bin_format format(1) shingles_size(1) threshold bcsize(separated by ,) output_dir/outfileprefix num_shingles
	- format 1 is a binary file of the type given in the example
	- shingle_size is the number of edges considered for hashing (I used 1 for web graph, a higher number could be better for text)
	- threshold (below this number vnmextract starts getting graph of the next smaller bcsize)
	- bcsize. Dense subgraph size considered for extraction. On web graphs I used 500,100,50,30,15,6.
	- output_dir/outfileprefix is the output and file directory (directory must exist)
	- num_shingles. Number of hashes considered (P) to make a matrix of hashes of P*|V|. I used 4.
	input: the input graph in binary (built with txtToBin) 
	output: dense subgraphs for each iteration there is a file with dense subgraph of the complete bipartite graph in text 
		format. Each line has the left and right set separated by a "-". It also saves a remaining graph each time it
		changes from one bcsize to the next.
	
	- scripts
        	- txtToBin: takes a graph in txt format (eu-2005-adj.txt), with node: <ordered outlinks separated by spaces>,
                 nodes without outlinks not included. It generates a binary graph eu-2005-adj.txt.bin
		- compile: g++ -o txtToBin txtToBin.cpp

3. Test it 
	- data directory contains a text.txt graph and eu-2005

4. Examples

   Considering an input graph in text format with node: ordered outlinks (without nodes with no outlinks). Nodes starts at 1.
   example on test.txt and eu-2005-adj.txt:

example 1.	- txtToBin test.txt 13 49
		- vnmextract test.txt.bin 1 1 2 4 out/test 4

example 2.	- txtToBin eu-2005-adj.txt 790989 19235140
		- vnmextract eu-2005-adj.txt.bin 1 1 10 500,100,50,30,15,6 out/eu-2005-adj 4

   Dense subgraphs in out/ directory with names test-biclique-it-iternumber
        remainder at bcsizes are in test-it-iternumber in the case of the test.txt.bin graph

5. Results.
   - As as described in the paper, we add self-loops ( edges (u,u)) on each adjacency list and then look for dense subgraphs.
   - The discovery algorithm is based on heuristics.

